\section{Introduction}

This is the literate source of vim-noweb, a Vim plugin that highlights
noweb files---files like this one.  A noweb source is a \TeX{} document
interrupted by code chunks: a chunk is introduced by [[@<<name@>>=]] in
the first column, ended by an [[@]] followed by a space or newline (or
implicitly by the next chunk definition), and chunks refer to each
other with [[@<<name@>>]].  The plugin highlights the prose as \TeX{},
marks the noweb markup---chunk headers, chunk references and
[[[[...]]]] code quotes---and highlights each code chunk's body in the
chunk's own language, which it infers the same way noweb's
\enquote{autolang} filter does.

The plugin's lineage, version by version:

\begin{description}
\item[v1.0--v1.2] the original \texttt{nw.vim} of Xun Gong and Dirk
  Baechle, mirrored from vim.org script 2129.
\item[v1.3] per-chunk language inference, ported from noweb's
  \enquote{autolang} filter.
\item[v1.4] reliable highlighting: defences against the included
  language syntaxes and against runaway \TeX{} regions.  The
  rationale for those defences is the bulk of this document.
\item[v1.5] language seeds come from the editor's own filetype
  database---on Neovim and Vim alike (\cref{sec:askeditor}); the
  fixed tables remain as a last resort.
\item[v1.6] editing support: chunk names complete and navigate
  through the buffer's own omnifunc and tagfunc
  (\cref{sec:navigation}).
\item[v1.6.1] the YCM trigger registers at startup rather than at
  FileType time (\cref{sec:ycmtrigger}); undefined names complete
  first at definition positions.
\end{description}

Versions 1.3 and 1.4 together amount to a complete rewrite: virtually
no code from v1.2 or earlier remains, only the file layout and the
group names.

Everything in this repository tangles from this file.  The plugin's
six files are the root chunks, named after their paths in the
plugin tree:

\begin{itemize}
\item [[syntax/noweb.vim]],
\item [[autoload/noweb.vim]],
\item [[ftdetect/noweb.vim]],
\item [[ftplugin/noweb.vim]],
\item [[plugin/noweb.vim]], and
\item [[lua/noweb/scan.lua]];
\end{itemize}

the [[LICENSE]] tangles from here as well
(\cref{sec:building}).  The tangled files are committed
alongside this source---a deliberate deviation from the usual
never-commit-generated rule, because a Vim plugin must work from a
bare [[git clone]]; plugin managers do not run notangle.
\Cref{sec:building} describes the build and the conventions this file
itself must follow (notably: every literal [[<<]] or [[>>]] inside a
code chunk is escaped as [[@<<]] and [[@>>]]).

One constraint shaped the initial literate conversion: the tangled
files had to reproduce the then hand-written files byte for byte, so
that [[git diff]] could prove the conversion changed nothing.  That
constraint served its purpose and has been lifted; this document is
now the only source, and the code chunks---comments included---evolve
here.

\section{Overview}
\label{sec:overview}

The plugin is six small files with a one-way control flow:

\begin{description}
\item[\texttt{plugin/noweb.vim}] runs once at startup and registers
  the noweb trigger with YCM before YCM snapshots its options
  (\cref{sec:ycmtrigger}).
\item[\texttt{ftdetect/noweb.vim}] tells Vim that [[*.nw]] buffers
  have filetype [[noweb]].
\item[\texttt{ftplugin/noweb.vim}] runs when that filetype is set and
  installs the buffer-local editing hooks: chunk names complete
  through the buffer's omnifunc and resolve as tags through its
  tagfunc (\cref{sec:navigation}).
\item[\texttt{syntax/noweb.vim}] runs when that filetype's syntax is
  requested.  It loads Vim's \TeX{} syntax as the base---the whole
  buffer is prose by default---then defines the noweb markup items and
  a \emph{fallback} region for code chunks, whose bodies it leaves
  unhighlighted.  Finally it calls [[noweb#refresh()]].
\item[\texttt{autoload/noweb.vim}] provides [[noweb#refresh()]]: scan
  the buffer, infer each chunk's language, include the needed language
  syntaxes, and layer one language-aware region per language on top of
  the fallback.  It re-runs on every write, so the highlighting
  follows the file as it evolves.  It also provides the completion
  and navigation functions the ftplugin points at.
\item[\texttt{lua/noweb/scan.lua}] is the occurrence scan those
  functions share, in Lua for speed; on Vim a Vim-script fallback in
  the autoload file takes its place (\cref{sec:luascan}).
\end{description}

Two invariants make the layering work, and almost every design
decision below traces back to one of them:

\begin{description}
\item[Definition order is priority.]  When several syntax items can
  start at the same position, the \emph{last defined} wins
  (\texttt{:h syn-priority}).  We exploit this deliberately, twice:
  the language-aware regions are built after the fallback region, so
  a chunk header starts a language region rather than the fallback
  (\cref{sec:fallback,sec:refresh}); and the item marking chunk
  references inside code is redefined after every included language,
  so it beats that language's lookalike patterns (\cref{sec:refresh}).
\item[Included syntaxes are hostile guests.]  [[:syntax include]]
  imports another filetype's rules wholesale, and some of them misfire
  on noweb markup.  The shell syntax, for instance, reads [[@<<word]]
  as the start of a here-document string---on a chunk header line
  that used to paint the whole chunk body as one string.  Sync rules
  come along too, and they are buffer-global: they tell Vim where it
  may restart parsing when scrolling, and the imported rules made it
  restart as if mid-chunk lines were top-level prose.  The defences
  are [[matchgroup]] on chunk headers (\cref{sec:fallback}), the
  late-defined chunk-reference item, and discarding every imported
  sync rule in favour of syncing from the start of the file (both in
  \cref{sec:refresh}).
\end{description}

There is a third, external adversary: the \TeX{} base syntax itself
can open a multi-line region in prose (an optional argument, a group)
that swallows everything that follows, chunk headers included.  The
chunk regions therefore carry [[containedin=tex.*]] so a header
\emph{always} starts a chunk, and a small recovery item closes stolen
[[[[...]]]] quotes (\cref{sec:quoting,sec:orphan}).

\section{Filetype detection}

Vim sources every [[ftdetect]] file on startup.  We use
[[setfiletype]] rather than [[set filetype=]]: [[setfiletype]] is a
no-op when the filetype is already set, so a user autocmd that assigns
the filetype itself (as the author's vimrc has done for years) keeps
working unchanged, whichever of the two fires first.

<<[[ftdetect/noweb.vim]]>>=
" setfiletype yields if the filetype is already set, so this coexists with
" any user autocmd doing the same.
autocmd BufRead,BufNewFile *.nw setfiletype noweb
@

\section{The syntax file}

The file reads top to bottom in the order Vim executes it: guard,
\TeX{} base, prose-level markup, the fallback chunk region, highlight
links, and finally the hand-over to the language-inference machinery.

<<[[syntax/noweb.vim]]>>=
<<syntax file header>>

<<bail out if a syntax is already active>>


<<load the \TeX{} base syntax>>

<<quoting and chunk-name markup>>

<<recover quoting stolen by \TeX{} option arguments>>

<<fallback code-chunk region>>

<<mark text-chunk starts>>

<<default highlight links>>

<<language-inference bootstrap>>
@

