The guard is the standard syntax-file idiom: if some syntax is already
active for the buffer, a second [[:runtime syntax/noweb.vim]] must not
redefine everything on top of it.  The [[version < 600]] branch is
fossil compatibility kept verbatim from v1.2.

<<bail out if a syntax is already active>>=
" Remove any old syntax stuff hanging around
if version < 600
  syntax clear
elseif exists("b:current_syntax")
  finish
endif
@

\subsection{The \TeX{} base}

Like CWEB's [[cweb.vim]], we treat the whole file as \TeX{} and punch
the code chunks out of it, so there is no \enquote{documentation
region}---prose is simply the top level.  The [[unlet]] afterwards
matters: [[tex.vim]] sets [[b:current_syntax]], and if we left it set,
our own guard-style checks further down the runtime path would think
the buffer already done; we are not finished defining the syntax yet.

<<load the \TeX{} base syntax>>=
" Like in CWEB, a NOWEB source file is treated as a TeX file
" including code chunks in between.
if version < 600
  source <sfile>:p:h/tex.vim
else
  runtime! syntax/tex.vim
  unlet b:current_syntax
endif
@

\subsection{Quoting and chunk names in prose}
\label{sec:quoting}

Noweb prose quotes code as [[[[...]]]] and may mention chunks as
[[@<<name@>>]]; both get their own items.  Two details are load-bearing:

\begin{description}
\item[ [[oneline]]: ] an unbalanced [[[[]] in prose used to open a
  region that ran to the end of the file and painted everything after
  it as [[Constant]].  Noweb's quoting is one-line in practice, so
  bounding the item costs nothing and removes the failure mode.
\item[ [[containedin=tex.*]]: ] these items are top-level, but \TeX{}
  regions opened in prose (optional arguments, groups, math) would
  otherwise hide the markup inside them.  Being injectable into every
  [[tex]]-prefixed group keeps the markup visible wherever prose ended
  up.  The v1.3 code named [[tex.*Zone]] groups here---groups which
  vimtex, the \TeX{} syntax actually loaded on this machine, does not
  even define.
\end{description}

<<quoting and chunk-name markup>>=
" containedin=tex.* lets these match even when a tex region has swallowed
" the surrounding prose (e.g. an unclosed optional-argument [ ... ), which
" vimtex can open across lines via nextgroup chains.
syn region  nowebTT     start="\[\["hs=s+2 end="\]\]"he=e-2 oneline containedin=tex.*
syn region  nowebName   start="@<<" end="@>>" oneline contains=nowebTT containedin=tex.*
@

\subsection{Recovering quoting stolen by option arguments}
\label{sec:orphan}

One adversary cannot be beaten by definition order: [[nextgroup]].
After [[\begin{quote}]] or [[\item]], the \TeX{} syntax expects an
optional argument and claims the next [[[]] via a [[nextgroup]]
chain---and a [[nextgroup]] match outranks \emph{every} ordinary item,
so [[nowebTT]] never gets to compete for the first bracket of a
[[[[...]]]] quote there.  If the quoted text then contains a \%, the
\TeX{} comment rule eats the closing brackets and the option region
stays open---in one real file it swallowed 147 lines of prose and
several chunks.

We recover \emph{inside} the stolen bracket instead, where ordinary
priority applies again: this contained match consumes the orphaned
[[[content]]] (spanning any \%, because an item that starts earlier
beats one that starts later), so scanning resumes exactly at the
trailing [[]]] and the option region closes on the same line.  The
lookahead is the safety catch: only a [[[X]]]]-shaped text---noweb
quoting seen from inside a stolen bracket---matches, never a genuine
\LaTeX{} [[[options]]] argument.

<<recover quoting stolen by \TeX{} option arguments>>=
" When [[...]] quoting follows a tex command taking an optional argument
" (\begin{env}, \item, ...), the tex syntax claims the first [ as the
" argument's opening delimiter via a nextgroup chain, which has priority
" over any ordinary item -- and a % inside the quoting then comments out
" the ]], leaving the option region open for the rest of the file.
" Recover from inside the stolen bracket: consume the orphaned [content]
" (which only exists in [X]] shapes, hence the lookahead) so the trailing
" ] closes the option region on the same line.
syn match nowebTTOrphan /\[[^][]*\]\]\@=/hs=s+1,he=e-1 contained containedin=tex.*Opt,tex.*Label
@

\subsection{The fallback chunk region}
\label{sec:fallback}

This region is the skeleton's safety net: it matches \emph{every}
chunk, leaves the body unhighlighted, and is outranked by the
language-aware regions that \cref{sec:refresh} layers on top (they are
defined later; definition order is priority).  Its shape, however, is
exactly the shape those regions reuse, so the reasoning lives here.

\begin{description}
\item[ [[matchgroup=nowebName]] on the start:] the header line gets
  chunk-name colours, and---the real point---contained items cannot
  match inside [[matchgroup]] text.  The shell syntax's here-doc rule
  matches [[@<<word]]; without the matchgroup it fired on the header
  itself and painted the whole chunk as one unterminated here-doc
  string.
\item[Two end patterns:] a chunk ends at the next text chunk
  ([[@]] plus space or newline) \emph{or} implicitly at the next chunk
  definition---exactly the two terminators notangle accepts.  A tab
  after [[@]] is deliberately \emph{not} an end: notangle treats that
  line as code, and the highlighting should not diverge from the
  tangler.
\item[ [[me=s-1]] on both ends:] the terminator itself stays
  unconsumed, so the following item---[[nowebStartText]] or the next
  chunk's region---starts on that very line.  (The v1.3 code used
  [[me=e-3]] on a two-character match and relied on Vim clamping the
  offset; it happened to work.)
\item[ [[keepend]]: ] included items must not push past the end match;
  without it a multi-line string in a chunk body could swallow the
  terminator.
\item[ [[containedin=tex.*]]: ] a chunk header must \emph{always} start
  a chunk, even when a runaway \TeX{} region (previous subsection) has
  swallowed the surrounding prose.  This is safe because the \TeX{}
  option regions lack [[keepend]], so a contained chunk region
  legitimately obscures their end patterns---a [[]]] inside code does
  not chop the chunk.
\end{description}

<<fallback code-chunk region>>=
" NOWEB code chunks are defined by @<<chunk_name@>>= and ended by the next
" "@" (not a "@@"!) in the first column of a line, or implicitly by the
" next chunk definition.  Both ends use me=s-1 so the terminator itself is
" left for the following item (nowebStartText or the next chunk region).
" The header is highlighted via matchgroup, which also keeps items of an
" included language syntax from matching inside it.  This is the fallback
" region for chunks whose language could not be inferred: its body is
" left unhighlighted (chunk references are still marked).  Language-aware
" regions are layered on top of it by noweb#refresh() below, which is why
" they take priority -- they are defined later.  nowebChunkRef is also
" defined there, after the language includes, so that it outranks them.
" containedin=tex.* guarantees a chunk header always starts a chunk, even
" inside a runaway tex region (see nowebTT above); the tex regions lack
" keepend, so a contained chunk safely obscures their end patterns.
syntax region nowebCode
      \ matchgroup=nowebName start=/^@<<.\{-}@>>=\s*$/
      \ matchgroup=NONE
      \ end=/^@\%( \|$\)/me=s-1
      \ end=/^@<<.\{-}@>>=\s*$/me=s-1
      \ keepend
      \ contains=nowebChunkRef,@NoSpell
      \ containedin=tex.*
@

\subsection{Text-chunk starts and highlight links}

The [[@]] that opens a text chunk gets a marker of its own---the ends
above left it unconsumed for exactly this item.

<<mark text-chunk starts>>=
" Here, we mark the beginning of a new text chunk.
syntax match nowebStartText /^@\%( \|$\)/ containedin=tex.*
@

The links are plain [[hi def link]] with no guard around them.  The
v1.2 code wrapped them in a global [[did_noweb_syntax_inits]] flag, a
pre-Vim-6 relic with a modern cost: after [[:syntax off | syntax on]]
the syntax file re-runs but the flag is still set, so the links were
never restored and the noweb colours silently vanished for the rest of
the session.  [[hi def link]] is idempotent; the guard protected
nothing.

<<default highlight links>>=
" The default methods for highlighting. Can be overridden later.
hi def link nowebStartText Constant
hi def link nowebTT     Constant
hi def link nowebTTOrphan nowebTT
hi def link nowebName     Type
hi def link nowebChunkRef nowebName
@

\subsection{Bootstrapping the language inference}

The rest of the file hands over to \cref{sec:autoload}.  The two
buffer variables are reset here, unconditionally: a full re-source
(e.g.\ [[:edit]]) starts the syntax from scratch, wiping the included
clusters, so stale bookkeeping claiming a language is already included
would make [[contains=@nowebLang_x]] silently match nothing.  Refresh
then runs once immediately and again after every write; the
buffer-local [[:NowebSyncLang]] covers everything in between.

<<language-inference bootstrap>>=
let b:current_syntax = "noweb"

" Infer chunk languages and build the per-language regions, then keep them
" up to date: on every load (here), after each write, and on demand via
" :NowebSyncLang.  A full re-source (e.g. :edit) wipes included clusters,
" so reset the bookkeeping before the initial inference.
let b:noweb_included = {}
let b:noweb_lang_groups = []
call noweb#refresh()

command! -buffer NowebSyncLang call noweb#refresh()

augroup nowebLangSync
  autocmd! * <buffer>
  autocmd BufWritePost <buffer> call noweb#refresh()
augroup END
@

\section{Language inference}
\label{sec:autoload}

This file is a Vimscript port of the \emph{algorithm} at the core of
noweb's \enquote{autolang} filter (from \texttt{tominted.nw}): seed
every chunk with a language taken from a filename-like chunk name or a
[[#!]] line, then propagate those languages along \emph{use} edges, so
a descriptively-named chunk used by a typed root inherits the root's
language.  Extension-only dispatch would miss most chunks in a real
document---the whole point of noweb is that chunks are named for what
they mean, not what file they land in.

The seeding \emph{data}, however, is not ported: on Neovim the
questions \enquote{what language is a file with this name?} and
\enquote{what language does this shebang name?} are answered by the
editor's own filetype database (\cref{sec:askeditor}), which is
exactly the data we would otherwise re-encode by hand.  The small
fixed tables that seeded versions 1.3--1.4 remain only as a fallback
for editors without that database.

The file reads data first, then helpers, then the algorithm---which
happens to be pedagogical order as well:

<<[[autoload/noweb.vim]]>>=
<<autoload file header>>

<<[[s:lexers_by_name]] table>>

<<[[s:lexers_by_ext]] table>>

<<[[s:interpreters]] table>>

<<the [[s:CONFLICT]] sentinel>>

<<[[s:filetype_match]]>>

<<[[s:detect_in_scratch]]>>

<<[[s:lexer_for_chunk]]>>

<<[[s:shebang_lang]]>>

<<[[s:re_escape]]>>

<<[[s:infer_languages]]>>

<<[[s:include_lang]]>>

<<[[noweb#refresh]]>>

<<the [[s:DEF]] and [[s:REF]] patterns>>

<<[[s:refs_on_line]]>>

<<[[s:chunk_occurrences]]>>

<<[[s:chunk_at_cursor]]>>

<<[[noweb#complete]]>>

<<[[noweb#tagfunc]]>>

<<[[noweb#next_occurrence]]>>

<<[[noweb#refs]]>>

<<[[noweb#occurrences]] and [[noweb#languages]] accessors>>
@

<<autoload file header>>=
" autoload/noweb.vim -- per-chunk language inference for noweb sources
"
" Ports the algorithm of the "autolang" filter from noweb's tominted.nw:
" every code chunk is seeded with a language from a filename-like chunk
" name or a #! line, and those languages then propagate along @<<use@>>
" edges (a worklist with conflict detection), so a descriptively-named
" chunk used by a typed root inherits the root's language.  On Neovim
" the seeds come from the editor's own filetype database
" (vim.filetype.match); fixed tables remain as the fallback.  The result
" drives one :syntax region per language, each including that language's
" own syntax file on demand.
@

\subsection{Asking the editor first}
\label{sec:askeditor}

Everything this file wants to know about names and shebangs is data
the editor already maintains, mapping filenames \emph{and} file
contents to filetypes---and filetypes are precisely the names
[[:syntax include]] wants.  Both Vim lineages can be asked.  Neovim
exposes its database as a function, [[vim.filetype.match]]; Vim keeps
the same database in autocommand form (the [[filetypedetect]] group:
[[filetype.vim]], plus [[scripts.vim]] for shebangs), which cannot be
called---but it can be \emph{run}, against a throwaway buffer.
Asking the editor instead of keeping our own tables removes the
redundancy, keeps the answers in sync with the editor across
upgrades, and widens coverage to every language the editor can
detect---[[x.zsh]] becomes [[zsh]], not the [[sh]] our table used to
approximate it with.

Edit-time inference may now disagree with weave-time autolang in
corner cases (autolang asks Pygments, which calls the same chunk
[[bash]]); that is by design, not drift: each side answers how
\emph{its} medium highlights the chunk.

The results are cached because chunk names recur on every refresh and
the database does not change within a session.  Only when there is no
database at all---filetype detection switched off, or a Vim too old
to have the group---does the function answer [['']] and route every
caller to its fallback table.

<<[[s:filetype_match]]>>=
" Ask the editor's filetype database for ARGS, e.g. {'filename': ...}
" or {'filename': ..., 'contents': [...]}.  Returns '' when there is
" no answer or no database to ask.
let s:ft_cache = {}
function! s:filetype_match(args) abort
  let l:key = string(a:args)
  if !has_key(s:ft_cache, l:key)
    if has('nvim')
      let s:ft_cache[l:key] = luaeval('vim.filetype.match(_A) or ""', a:args)
    elseif exists('#filetypedetect#BufRead')
      let s:ft_cache[l:key] = s:detect_in_scratch(a:args)
    else
      let s:ft_cache[l:key] = ''
    endif
  endif
  return s:ft_cache[l:key]
endfunction
@

On Vim the query is a small performance: create a throwaway buffer
whose name is the candidate filename---under a session-unique
directory that does not exist, so no real file or buffer can
collide---give it the shebang line as contents, and fire the
[[filetypedetect]] autocommands at it.  [[eventignore]] does the
chaperoning.  Everything is suppressed while the buffer comes and
goes, so no other plugin reacts to a buffer that lives for a
millisecond; during the detection itself only [[FileType]] stays
suppressed, because the detection must be free to assign
[[&filetype]]---that assignment is the answer we read---but nothing
should start loading ftplugins over it.  The cache above makes the
cost irrelevant: one throwaway buffer per unique question per
session.

<<[[s:detect_in_scratch]]>>=
" Vim's counterpart of vim.filetype.match: run the filetypedetect
" autocommands against a throwaway buffer with the candidate name and
" contents, and read the &filetype they assign.
let s:scratch_dir = tempname()
function! s:detect_in_scratch(args) abort
  let l:ft = ''
  let l:eventignore = &eventignore
  set eventignore=all
  try
    silent execute 'keepalt new'
          \ fnameescape(s:scratch_dir . '/' . get(a:args, 'filename', 'noweb-chunk'))
    setlocal buftype=nofile noswapfile
    if has_key(a:args, 'contents')
      call setline(1, a:args['contents'])
    endif
    set eventignore=FileType
    silent doautocmd filetypedetect BufRead
    let l:ft = &l:filetype
  finally
    set eventignore=all
    silent! bwipeout!
    let &eventignore = l:eventignore
  endtry
  return l:ft
endfunction
@

\subsection{Naming a chunk's language}

The fallback tables carry the name-based seeding when the editor's
database is unavailable: exact basenames (the Makefile family has no
extension) and extensions.  The values are Vim syntax names, which
double as the canonical language keys everywhere else in this file.

<<[[s:lexers_by_name]] table>>=
" Chunk name (verbatim basename) -> Vim syntax name.
let s:lexers_by_name = {
      \ 'Makefile': 'make', 'makefile': 'make', 'GNUmakefile': 'make',
      \ }
@

<<[[s:lexers_by_ext]] table>>=
" Chunk name extension -> Vim syntax name (the canonical language key).
let s:lexers_by_ext = {
      \ '.py': 'python', '.pyw': 'python',
      \ '.c': 'c', '.h': 'c',
      \ '.cpp': 'cpp', '.cc': 'cpp', '.cxx': 'cpp', '.C': 'cpp',
      \ '.hpp': 'cpp', '.hh': 'cpp', '.hxx': 'cpp',
      \ '.sh': 'sh', '.bash': 'sh', '.zsh': 'sh', '.ksh': 'sh',
      \ '.ml': 'ocaml', '.mli': 'ocaml',
      \ '.mk': 'make',
      \ '.pl': 'perl', '.pm': 'perl',
      \ '.rb': 'ruby', '.lua': 'lua', '.php': 'php',
      \ '.js': 'javascript', '.mjs': 'javascript', '.ts': 'typescript',
      \ '.java': 'java', '.r': 'r', '.R': 'r',
      \ '.vim': 'vim', '.awk': 'awk', '.sed': 'sed',
      \ '.hs': 'haskell', '.go': 'go', '.rs': 'rust',
      \ '.sql': 'sql', '.html': 'html', '.css': 'css',
      \ '.tex': 'tex', '.yml': 'yaml', '.yaml': 'yaml',
      \ }
@

The lookup mirrors autolang's [[lexer_for_chunk]]: a chunk name may
use noweb's [[[[...]]]] quoting and may contain directory separators,
and only the basename decides---[[@<<src/util.py@>>]] is Python no
matter where it tangles to.  Note the quote-stripping must come
first; [[[[Makefile]]]] should be looked up as a basename.  The
basename, not the full name, also goes to the editor's database: its
path-based patterns (matching e.g.\ files under [[etc/]]) make no
sense for chunk names.

<<[[s:lexer_for_chunk]]>>=
" Return the Vim syntax name for chunk NAME, or '' if its name says nothing.
" The name may use noweb's [[...]] quoting and may contain directory
" separators; only the basename decides (mirrors lexer_for_chunk).
function! s:lexer_for_chunk(name) abort
  let l:name = a:name
  if l:name =~# '^\[\[.*\]\]$'
    let l:name = l:name[2:-3]
  endif
  let l:base = matchstr(l:name, '[^/]*$')
  let l:ft = s:filetype_match({'filename': l:base})
  if l:ft !=# ''
    return l:ft
  endif
  <<look [[l:base]] up in the fallback tables>>
endfunction
@

<<look [[l:base]] up in the fallback tables>>=
if has_key(s:lexers_by_name, l:base)
  return s:lexers_by_name[l:base]
endif
let l:dot = strridx(l:base, '.')
if l:dot >= 0
  let l:ext = strpart(l:base, l:dot)
  if has_key(s:lexers_by_ext, l:ext)
    return s:lexers_by_ext[l:ext]
  endif
endif
return ''
@

\subsection{Shebangs}

A chunk whose name says nothing may still open with [[#!/bin/sh]].
On Neovim the shebang goes to the editor's database as file
\emph{contents}, together with a neutral, extensionless dummy
filename---contents alone make Neovim skip parts of its detection
(a bare [[#!/bin/bash]] goes unrecognised), while an unknown filename
routes the query through the full machinery, shebang analysis
included.

The fallback interpreter table maps interpreter basenames to syntax
names, and its parser follows the [[env]] indirection because
[[#!/usr/bin/env python3]] is the portable spelling---the interesting
token is the argument, not [[env]] itself.

<<[[s:interpreters]] table>>=
" Interpreter basename from a #! line -> Vim syntax name.
let s:interpreters = {
      \ 'python': 'python', 'python2': 'python', 'python3': 'python',
      \ 'sh': 'sh', 'bash': 'sh', 'dash': 'sh', 'ksh': 'sh', 'zsh': 'sh',
      \ 'perl': 'perl', 'ruby': 'ruby', 'lua': 'lua', 'php': 'php',
      \ 'node': 'javascript', 'nodejs': 'javascript',
      \ 'awk': 'awk', 'gawk': 'awk', 'sed': 'sed', 'Rscript': 'r',
      \ }
@

<<[[s:shebang_lang]]>>=
" Return the language a #! line names, or '' if it is not a #! line.
function! s:shebang_lang(line) abort
  if a:line !~# '^#!'
    return ''
  endif
  let l:ft = s:filetype_match({'filename': 'noweb-chunk', 'contents': [a:line]})
  if l:ft !=# ''
    return l:ft
  endif
  <<parse the shebang with the fallback table>>
endfunction
@

<<parse the shebang with the fallback table>>=
" Follow the env indirection: the interesting token is env's argument.
let l:toks = split(a:line[2:])
if empty(l:toks)
  return ''
endif
let l:interp = matchstr(l:toks[0], '[^/]*$')
if l:interp ==# 'env' && len(l:toks) > 1
  let l:interp = matchstr(l:toks[1], '[^/]*$')
endif
return get(s:interpreters, l:interp, '')
@

\subsection{Escaping chunk names into patterns}

The region builder in \cref{sec:refresh} splices chunk names into a
magic-mode [[/.../]] pattern, so everything magic there must be
escaped---including the backslash first, so the escapes themselves do
not get re-escaped.

<<[[s:re_escape]]>>=
" Escape a chunk name for literal use inside a /.../ (magic) pattern.
function! s:re_escape(name) abort
  return escape(a:name, '\/.*$^~[]')
endfunction
@

\subsection{Inferring the languages}
\label{sec:infer}

The inference is one buffer scan followed by a propagation pass over
the collected graph:

<<[[s:infer_languages]]>>=
" Scan the buffer and return {chunk name -> Vim syntax name}.
function! s:infer_languages() abort
  <<scan chunks for language seeds and uses>>
  <<propagate languages along use edges>>
  return l:langs
endfunction
@

\subsubsection{The scan}

The scanner is a small state machine over the buffer lines: a chunk
header enters code mode, a text-chunk marker leaves it, and everything
in between belongs to the current chunk.  The header and terminator
patterns are the same ones the syntax regions use---inference and
highlighting must agree on what a chunk is.

The state is a handful of variables serving two different masters.
Two of them are the scan's \emph{products}: [[l:langs]] maps chunk
names to inferred languages and is what the function returns, and
[[l:uses]] is the graph the propagation pass will walk---for each
chunk, the set of chunk names its body references.  One is a
\emph{protection marker}: [[l:explicit]] records which entries of
[[l:langs]] came from the chunk's own name or shebang, so that
propagation later refuses to override an author-stated language with
an inherited one.  The rest is the state machine proper:
[[l:current]] and [[l:in_code]] say which chunk we are inside, if
any, and [[l:body_started]] remembers whether a non-blank body line
has been seen yet---only the first one may carry a shebang.

<<scan chunks for language seeds and uses>>=
let l:lines = getline(1, '$')
let l:uses = {}
let l:langs = {}
let l:explicit = {}
let l:current = ''
let l:in_code = 0
let l:body_started = 0
for l:line in l:lines
  if l:line =~# '^@<<.\{-}@>>=\s*$'
    <<open a chunk and seed its language from its name>>
  elseif l:in_code && l:line =~# '^@\($\| \)'
    let l:in_code = 0
    let l:current = ''
  elseif l:in_code && l:current !=# ''
    <<seed from a shebang on the first body line>>
    <<collect chunk uses on the line>>
  endif
endfor
@

A name-derived language is recorded as \emph{explicit}: the name is
the author's own statement of intent, so neither a later shebang nor
propagation may override it.

<<open a chunk and seed its language from its name>>=
let l:current = matchstr(l:line, '^@<<\zs.\{-}\ze@>>=\s*$')
let l:in_code = 1
let l:body_started = 0
if !has_key(l:uses, l:current)
  let l:uses[l:current] = {}
endif
if !has_key(l:explicit, l:current)
  let l:lx = s:lexer_for_chunk(l:current)
  if l:lx !=# ''
    let l:langs[l:current] = l:lx
    let l:explicit[l:current] = 1
  endif
endif
@

Only the first \emph{non-blank} body line can be a shebang---that is
where a kernel would look, modulo the blank lines notangle strips
between concatenated chunk definitions.

<<seed from a shebang on the first body line>>=
if !l:body_started && l:line !~# '^\s*$'
  let l:body_started = 1
  if !has_key(l:explicit, l:current)
    let l:sb = s:shebang_lang(l:line)
    if l:sb !=# ''
      let l:langs[l:current] = l:sb
      let l:explicit[l:current] = 1
    endif
  endif
endif
@

Uses are collected from every code line.  A [[@<<...@>>]] immediately
followed by [[=]] is a definition, not a use---continued definitions
of the same chunk would otherwise register the chunk as using itself,
which is harmless for propagation but pollutes the graph.

<<collect chunk uses on the line>>=
" Collect @<<use@>> references (a @<< ... @>> not followed by '=').
let l:start = 0
while 1
  let l:mp = matchstrpos(l:line, '@<<.\{-}@>>', l:start)
  if l:mp[1] < 0
    break
  endif
  let l:mend = l:mp[2]
  if l:mend < len(l:line) && l:line[l:mend] ==# '='
    let l:start = l:mend
    continue
  endif
  let l:uses[l:current][l:mp[0][2:-3]] = 1
  let l:start = l:mend
endwhile
@

\subsubsection{Propagation}

Languages flow from seeded chunks to the chunks they use, worklist
style.  Two rules keep the result trustworthy.  Explicitly-typed
chunks are immune---their name or shebang outranks any inheritance.
And a chunk reachable from two \emph{differently}-typed parents is
marked conflicted rather than first-come-first-served: a wrong
highlight is worse than none, and conflicted chunks simply keep the
plain fallback region.

<<propagate languages along use edges>>=
" Propagate languages along use edges (worklist with conflict marking).
let l:worklist = keys(l:langs)
while !empty(l:worklist)
  let l:parent = remove(l:worklist, -1)
  let l:lang = l:langs[l:parent]
  if l:lang ==# s:CONFLICT
    continue
  endif
  for l:child in keys(get(l:uses, l:parent, {}))
    if has_key(l:explicit, l:child) || s:lexer_for_chunk(l:child) !=# ''
      continue
    endif
    if !has_key(l:langs, l:child)
      let l:langs[l:child] = l:lang
      call add(l:worklist, l:child)
    elseif l:langs[l:child] !=# s:CONFLICT && l:langs[l:child] !=# l:lang
      let l:langs[l:child] = s:CONFLICT
    endif
  endfor
endwhile
@

The conflict marker only needs to be impossible as a real syntax
name; the NUL bytes guarantee that.

<<the [[s:CONFLICT]] sentinel>>=
let s:CONFLICT = "\x00conflict\x00"
@

\subsection{Including a language's syntax}
\label{sec:include}

[[:syntax include]] reads a syntax file into a cluster instead of the
buffer's top level.  The dance around [[b:current_syntax]] is forced
on us: syntax files begin with the guard we ourselves use---bail out
if [[b:current_syntax]] is set---so the variable must be unset for the
include to do anything, and restored afterwards so the buffer still
knows its own syntax is loaded.  Absence of a syntax file is cached as
an empty cluster name; there is no point globbing the runtime path for
[[fortran.vim]] on every write.

<<[[s:include_lang]]>>=
" Include LANG's syntax under cluster @nowebLang_<lang>, once per buffer.
" Returns the cluster name, or '' if Vim ships no such syntax.
function! s:include_lang(lang) abort
  let l:cluster = 'nowebLang_' . substitute(a:lang, '[^A-Za-z0-9]', '_', 'g')
  if has_key(b:noweb_included, a:lang)
    return b:noweb_included[a:lang]
  endif
  if empty(globpath(&runtimepath, 'syntax/' . a:lang . '.vim'))
    let b:noweb_included[a:lang] = ''
    return ''
  endif
  let l:saved = exists('b:current_syntax') ? b:current_syntax : ''
  if l:saved !=# ''
    unlet b:current_syntax
  endif
  execute 'silent! syntax include @' . l:cluster . ' syntax/' . a:lang . '.vim'
  if l:saved !=# ''
    let b:current_syntax = l:saved
  elseif exists('b:current_syntax')
    unlet b:current_syntax
  endif
  let b:noweb_included[a:lang] = l:cluster
  return l:cluster
endfunction
@

\subsection{Rebuilding the regions}
\label{sec:refresh}

[[noweb#refresh()]] is the entry point the syntax file calls; it
re-infers everything and rebuilds all per-language items.  Its five
phases run in a fixed order, and the last two exist purely because of
the invariants of \cref{sec:overview}: the chunk-reference item must
be defined after every include, and the sync policy must be restated
after every include.

<<[[noweb#refresh]]>>=
" Re-infer chunk languages and (re)build the per-language syntax regions.
function! noweb#refresh() abort
  <<reset per-buffer region state>>

  <<bucket chunk names by language>>

  <<build one region per language>>

  <<redefine [[nowebChunkRef]] after the includes>>

  <<sync from the start>>
endfunction
@

The reset clears the previous refresh's regions by group name.
[[syntax clear]] on a group removes its patterns but keeps the group
id valid, so items already referring to the group do not break.

<<reset per-buffer region state>>=
if !exists('b:noweb_included')
  let b:noweb_included = {}
endif
if !exists('b:noweb_lang_groups')
  let b:noweb_lang_groups = []
endif
for l:grp in b:noweb_lang_groups
  execute 'silent! syntax clear ' . l:grp
endfor
let b:noweb_lang_groups = []
@

Regions are built per \emph{language}, not per chunk---a document with
forty Python chunks needs one region with forty start alternatives,
not forty regions.  Conflicted chunks are dropped here (they keep the
fallback), and so are two languages by name: [[text]] means
\enquote{explicitly no highlighting}, and [[tex]] is skipped because
the buffer's outer syntax already \emph{is} \TeX{}---re-including it
as a cluster would load the whole \TeX{} syntax a second time and
re-run its [[syn sync]] and [[spell]] setup mid-refresh.

<<bucket chunk names by language>>=
let l:langs = s:infer_languages()
let l:bylang = {}
for [l:name, l:lang] in items(l:langs)
  " 'tex' is skipped: the buffer's outer syntax already is tex, and
  " re-including it as a cluster re-runs its sync/spell setup mid-refresh.
  if l:lang ==# s:CONFLICT || l:lang ==# 'text' || l:lang ==# 'tex'
    continue
  endif
  let l:bylang[l:lang] = get(l:bylang, l:lang, []) + [l:name]
endfor
@

Each region is the fallback region's shape (\cref{sec:overview}
explains [[matchgroup]], the two [[me=s-1]] ends and [[keepend]]) with
the language's cluster added to [[contains]].  Being defined after
the fallback, these win the header-line tie against it.

<<build one region per language>>=
let l:i = 0
for [l:lang, l:names] in items(l:bylang)
  let l:cluster = s:include_lang(l:lang)
  if l:cluster ==# ''
    continue
  endif
  let l:i += 1
  let l:grp = 'nowebCodeLang' . l:i
  let l:alt = join(map(copy(l:names), 's:re_escape(v:val)'), '\|')
  " matchgroup= keeps included items (e.g. sh.vim's here-doc rule, which
  " matches @<<word) from ever firing on the header line; me=s-1 on both
  " ends leaves the terminator unconsumed, so a following chunk header
  " both ends this region and starts the next one.
  execute 'syntax region ' . l:grp
        \ . ' matchgroup=nowebName start=/^@<<\%(' . l:alt . '\)@>>=\s*$/'
        \ . ' matchgroup=NONE'
        \ . ' end=/^@\%( \|$\)/me=s-1'
        \ . ' end=/^@<<.\{-}@>>=\s*$/me=s-1'
        \ . ' keepend'
        \ . ' contains=@' . l:cluster . ',nowebChunkRef,@NoSpell'
        \ . ' containedin=tex.*'
  call add(b:noweb_lang_groups, l:grp)
endfor
@

Chunk references inside code bodies are the one noweb markup that must
survive \emph{inside} the included languages---and the shell syntax's
here-doc rule matches the same [[@<<]] text.  Winning that tie is why
this item is (re)defined here, after every include, rather than once
in the syntax file.  The pattern requires non-space first and last
name characters, so a real redirected here-doc like
[[cat @<<EOF @>> log]] is left to the shell syntax.

<<redefine [[nowebChunkRef]] after the includes>>=
" Must be (re)defined after every :syntax include above: at the same
" start position the last-defined item wins (:h syn-priority), which is
" what lets chunk refs beat e.g. shHereDoc's @<<-pattern inside sh chunks.
" First/last name chars must be non-space, so `cat @<<EOF @>> log` stays a
" here-doc.  syntax clear keeps the group id (contains= refs stay valid)
" while preventing pattern accumulation across refreshes.
silent! syntax clear nowebChunkRef
syntax match nowebChunkRef /@<<\%([^ ]\|[^ ].\{-}[^ ]\)@>>/ contained contains=nowebTT
@

Finally, the sync policy.  This line is the single biggest reliability
fix in v1.4: every included syntax file may install [[syn sync]] rules,
and those are \emph{buffer-global}---the Python syntax declares that a
line matching [[^def]] is top level, the make syntax declares that
nearly \emph{every} line is.  Scrolling then re-syncs the buffer
wrongly and chunks lose their highlighting mid-file, depending on
which language happened to be included last.  We discard all imported
sync rules and parse from the top of the file; noweb sources are small
enough that exactness costs nothing.

<<sync from the start>>=
" Included syntax files install buffer-global sync rules (pythonSync
" grouphere on ^def, make's groupthere on ^[^\t#], small minlines) that
" re-sync this buffer wrongly while scrolling.  Drop them and parse from
" the top; noweb sources are small.
syntax sync clear
syntax sync fromstart
@

\section{Completion and navigation}
\label{sec:navigation}

Up to v1.5 the plugin only ever \emph{showed} things.  This section
adds the editing side: completing chunk names, jumping to a chunk's
definition, and stepping through its appends and uses.

The guiding principle is the one that served the language inference
well (\cref{sec:askeditor}): hook the editor's native mechanisms
rather than invent parallel ones.  Completion is exposed as the
buffer's [['omnifunc']], which serves two masters at once: plain Vim
users get \texttt{CTRL-X CTRL-O} with no configuration, and
completion frameworks get their standard integration point---for a
filetype it has no native engine for, YCM falls back to the buffer's
omnifunc (its \enquote{omni completer}), and coc, nvim-cmp and
asyncomplete all ship omnifunc bridges.  Navigation is exposed as the
buffer's [['tagfunc']] (\cref{sec:tagfunc}), which similarly makes
the whole family of tag commands work unmapped.  One implementation,
every frontend.

\subsection{The filetype plugin}
\label{sec:ftplugin}

Both hooks are buffer-local options, and the conventional owner of
buffer-local options is a filetype plugin: Vim sources
[[ftplugin/noweb.vim]] whenever a buffer's filetype becomes
[[noweb]].  This is the plugin's fourth tangled file.  The
implementations live in the autoload file with the rest of the
machinery and load on first use; the ftplugin only points at them.

<<[[ftplugin/noweb.vim]]>>=
" ftplugin/noweb.vim -- completion and navigation for noweb sources
"
" Buffer-local hooks only: the implementations live in
" autoload/noweb.vim and load on first use.

if exists('b:did_ftplugin')
  finish
endif
let b:did_ftplugin = 1

<<hook completion and navigation into the buffer>>

<<the occurrence maps and the [[:NowebRefs]] command>>

<<start the shadow machinery>>
@

[[b:undo_ftplugin]] is the ftplugin counterpart of the syntax file's
idempotence concerns (\cref{sec:building}): when the filetype changes
away from [[noweb]], Vim runs it to undo everything we set.  The
[['tagfunc']] option is younger than the rest of what the plugin
relies on (Vim 8.2), hence the feature test---on an older Vim the
plugin degrades to completion only.

<<hook completion and navigation into the buffer>>=
setlocal omnifunc=noweb#complete
let b:undo_ftplugin = 'setlocal omnifunc<'
if exists('+tagfunc')
  setlocal tagfunc=noweb#tagfunc
  let b:undo_ftplugin .= ' tagfunc<'
endif
@

The remaining navigation surface is small: a motion pair stepping
through a chunk's occurrences (\texttt{]c} and \texttt{[c}, mirroring
Vim's bracket-motion family) and a command collecting them in the
location list.  The maps go through [[<Plug>]] indirection and yield
to the user twice over: a [[g:noweb_no_maps]] switch suppresses them
wholesale, and [[hasmapto]] skips any the user has already bound to
their own keys.  (\texttt{]c}/\texttt{[c} also mean
\enquote{next/previous change} in diff mode; diffed noweb sources are
rare, but remapping the [[<Plug>]]s moves ours out of the way.)

<<the occurrence maps and the [[:NowebRefs]] command>>=
command! -buffer NowebRefs call noweb#refs()

nnoremap <silent> <Plug>(noweb-next-occurrence)
      \ :<C-u>call noweb#next_occurrence(1)<CR>
nnoremap <silent> <Plug>(noweb-prev-occurrence)
      \ :<C-u>call noweb#next_occurrence(-1)<CR>
if !get(g:, 'noweb_no_maps', 0)
  if !hasmapto('<Plug>(noweb-next-occurrence)', 'n')
    nmap <buffer> ]c <Plug>(noweb-next-occurrence)
  endif
  if !hasmapto('<Plug>(noweb-prev-occurrence)', 'n')
    nmap <buffer> [c <Plug>(noweb-prev-occurrence)
  endif
endif

let b:undo_ftplugin .= ' | silent! nunmap <buffer> ]c'
      \ . ' | silent! nunmap <buffer> [c'
      \ . ' | silent! delcommand -buffer NowebRefs'
@

\subsection{Registering the YCM trigger at startup}
\label{sec:ycmtrigger}

YCM completes through the omnifunc, but fires it only when the typed
text matches a per-filetype \emph{trigger sequence}---and it has no
default trigger for this filetype.  Registering [[@<<]] makes the
popup appear exactly when a chunk reference begins.

Version 1.6 did this registration in the ftplugin, which is subtly
too late.  YCM sends every [[g:ycm_*]] option to its ycmd server
\emph{once}, when [[youcompleteme#Enable()]] runs at [[VimEnter]];
later changes are never seen.  An ftplugin runs when a buffer's
filetype is set, and for a noweb file opened at startup that is
before [[VimEnter]]---which made the bug intermittent: chunk
completion worked in sessions started as \texttt{vim file.nw} and
silently died for files opened later (what remained was YCM's
identifier collector, which only re-reads the buffer on events like
writes, so completion of new chunk names appeared to lag one
[[:w]] behind).

The registration therefore lives in the plugin's fifth tangled file,
[[plugin/noweb.vim]]: [[plugin]] files are sourced during startup,
always before [[VimEnter]], whatever order the plugin manager loads
them in---YCM defers its [[Enable()]] to [[VimEnter]] precisely so
that everything else has been sourced by then.  The [[has_key]]
guard keeps the registration polite: a user who has chosen their own
trigger, or disabled one, wins.  (Other frameworks need no help
here: their omnifunc bridges come with their own triggering
configuration.)

<<[[plugin/noweb.vim]]>>=
" plugin/noweb.vim -- startup-time configuration for noweb sources
"
" Runs before VimEnter, in particular before YCM snapshots its
" options for the ycmd server; buffer-local setup lives in
" ftplugin/noweb.vim instead.

if exists('g:loaded_noweb')
  finish
endif
let g:loaded_noweb = 1

<<register the chunk-reference trigger with YCM>>
@

<<register the chunk-reference trigger with YCM>>=
" YCM fires the omnifunc on a per-filetype trigger sequence and has no
" default for noweb; register @<< unless the user chose their own.
" This must run before VimEnter: YCM reads the option once, at startup.
let g:ycm_semantic_triggers = get(g:, 'ycm_semantic_triggers', {})
if !has_key(g:ycm_semantic_triggers, 'noweb')
  let g:ycm_semantic_triggers.noweb = ['@<<']
endif
@

\subsection{Scanning for occurrences}
\label{sec:occurrences}

Completion and navigation both need the same inventory: where is
every chunk defined (possibly several times---noweb definitions
accumulate), and where is every chunk used?  The language-inference
scan of \cref{sec:infer} walks the same lines but answers a different
question, tracking per-chunk language state; the two scans stay
separate so neither carries the other's bookkeeping.

The patterns are shared by everything in this section.  A definition
is a header alone on its line.  A reference is any [[@<<name@>>]]
whose brackets are not escaped---in code chunks a literal [[@<<]] is
written with a leading [[@]], and those escapes must not surface as
phantom chunks in the completion menu (the language scan can afford
to ignore this; a use edge from a phantom name simply never
propagates anywhere).  The name may contain blanks, but not start or
end with one, mirroring noweb's own markup.

Note what the reference pattern does \emph{not} contain: the
[[@]]-escape exclusion.  Expressing it as look-behinds
([[@\@1<!]]) reads nicely but pushes Vim's regex engine off its fast
path; the escape tests are instead two plain string comparisons next
to each match, in \cref{sec:refsonline}.  The third pattern is the
scan's skip list: a line can only matter to the scan if it contains
a bracket pair or starts a text chunk, and [[s:HOT]] matches exactly
those lines (definition headers contain [[@<<]], so the first
alternative covers them too).

<<the [[s:DEF]] and [[s:REF]] patterns>>=
" A chunk definition (nonempty name) alone on its line, and a chunk
" reference; both capture the name via \zs / \ze.  The @-escape
" exclusion is NOT in the reference pattern -- look-behinds are slow
" -- but tested by s:refs_on_line beside each match.  s:HOT matches
" every line the occurrence scan cannot skip.
let s:DEF = '^@<<\zs.\{-1,}\ze@>>=\s*$'
let s:REF = '@<<\zs\%([^ ]\|[^ ].\{-}[^ ]\)\ze@>>'
let s:HOT = '@<<\|^@\%( \|$\)'
@

The scanner is the same state machine as the language scan: a header
enters code mode, a text-chunk marker leaves it, and references are
collected only \emph{inside} code---in prose, [[@<<...@>>]] is just
text to noweb, so it must be just text to us.  Each occurrence is a
(line, column) pair pointing at the first bracket, ready for a jump.
When does the scan's cost strike?  Only when somebody asks---a
completion, a tag lookup, an occurrence jump---never on editor
events; and the scan always reads the live buffer, so unsaved chunks
are visible the moment they are typed.  A memo on [[b:changedtick]]
(which Vim bumps on every buffer modification) keeps repeated
queries from rescanning an unchanged buffer, so the price is one
rescan per edit-then-query, paid under a completion trigger.  (YCM
additionally caches the returned candidates for the duration of a
completion popup, so it asks once per triggered popup, not once per
keystroke.)  The rescan is deliberately \emph{not} incremental:
recomputing from scratch cannot go stale, where tracking edited
regions can.

That leaves making the full pass fast, which is a tale of three
implementations, benchmarked on the largest noweb source on the
author's disk (nytid's \texttt{todo.nw}, 15\,000 lines) and on this
document (1\,700 lines).  A naive Vim-script loop over every line
costs 43\,ms on this document---not any pattern's fault, but the
per-statement overhead of an interpreted loop body run 1\,700 times.
Hopping between candidate lines with [[match()]], which searches a
list in C, cuts that to 13\,ms---but on the 15\,000-line file still
costs about 100\,ms, now dominated by [[match()]] recompiling its
pattern on every call.  Legacy Vim script has nothing faster to
offer, so the scan does what the language seeding already does
(\cref{sec:askeditor}): it asks the editor.  On Neovim the scan runs
in Lua (\cref{sec:luascan})---2\,ms for the 15\,000-line file, about
4 end-to-end with the result's conversion back to Vim script,
imperceptible---and the [[match()]] loop remains as the fallback for
Vim, where the worst noweb sources in practice cost tens of
milliseconds, once per edit-then-query.

The dispatcher in [[s:chunk_occurrences]] chooses between the two
and normalizes the Lua result: [[luaeval]] converts an empty Lua table
to an empty \emph{dictionary}, so a chunk with uses but no
definitions (or vice versa) would come back with the wrong type
where a list is expected.  Setting [[g:noweb_scan_vimscript]] forces
the fallback on Neovim; the test suite uses it to hold the two
implementations to identical output, and it doubles as an escape
hatch.

<<[[s:chunk_occurrences]]>>=
" Scan the buffer and return {chunk name -> {'defs': [...], 'uses': [...]}}
" where every entry is a [lnum, col] pair (1-based, at the first <).
" A chunk defined n times has n defs -- appends are definitions too.
" Memoized on b:changedtick: the scan only re-runs after an edit.
" On Neovim the scan runs in Lua (lua/noweb/scan.lua); the Vim script
" loop is the fallback.  Both must produce identical results.
function! s:chunk_occurrences() abort
  if get(b:, 'noweb_occ_tick', -1) == b:changedtick
    return b:noweb_occ
  endif
  if has('nvim') && !get(g:, 'noweb_scan_vimscript', 0)
    let l:occ = luaeval('require("noweb.scan")()')
    " luaeval turns an empty Lua table into a dict; lists are expected.
    for l:e in values(l:occ)
      if type(l:e.defs) == v:t_dict
        let l:e.defs = []
      endif
      if type(l:e.uses) == v:t_dict
        let l:e.uses = []
      endif
    endfor
  else
    <<scan the buffer in Vim script>>
  endif
  let b:noweb_occ = l:occ
  let b:noweb_occ_tick = b:changedtick
  return l:occ
endfunction

function! s:entry(occ, name) abort
  if !has_key(a:occ, a:name)
    let a:occ[a:name] = {'defs': [], 'uses': []}
  endif
  return a:occ[a:name]
endfunction
@

\subsubsection{The scan in Vim script}
\label{sec:vimscan}

The fallback does not step through the buffer line by line: it asks
[[match()]] for the next line matching [[s:HOT]]---that search runs
in C---and interprets Vim script only on the lines that can matter.
Skipped lines cannot change the state machine's state, by
construction of [[s:HOT]].

<<scan the buffer in Vim script>>=
let l:occ = {}
let l:lines = getline(1, '$')
let l:in_code = 0
let l:i = match(l:lines, s:HOT)
while l:i >= 0
  let l:line = l:lines[l:i]
  let l:lnum = l:i + 1
  if l:line =~# s:DEF
    let l:in_code = 1
    call add(s:entry(l:occ, matchstr(l:line, s:DEF)).defs, [l:lnum, 1])
  elseif l:line =~# '^@\%( \|$\)'
    let l:in_code = 0
  elseif l:in_code
    <<record every reference on the line>>
  endif
  let l:i = match(l:lines, s:HOT, l:i + 1)
endwhile
@

A code line reaching the reference branch is known to contain a
bracket pair---that is what put it on the hot list.

<<record every reference on the line>>=
for l:ref in s:refs_on_line(l:line)
  call add(s:entry(l:occ, l:ref[0]).uses, [l:lnum, l:ref[1] - 1])
endfor
@

\subsubsection{References on one line}
\label{sec:refsonline}

[[matchstrpos]] returns the matched name with its start and end;
[[\zs]] puts the start two bytes past the opening bracket, hence the
arithmetic when checking the byte before the brackets and when
resuming the scan past the closing pair.  A match is discarded as
escaped when a [[@]] precedes its opening bracket or ends its
name (the latter means the closing bracket was escaped, [[@@>>]]).

<<[[s:refs_on_line]]>>=
" Chunk references on one line as [name, start, end] triples, with
" 0-based byte indices of the name between the brackets.  The @-escape
" tests live here as string comparisons; see s:REF.
function! s:refs_on_line(line) abort
  let l:refs = []
  let l:start = 0
  while 1
    let l:mp = matchstrpos(a:line, s:REF, l:start)
    if l:mp[1] < 0
      return l:refs
    endif
    if (l:mp[1] < 3 || a:line[l:mp[1] - 3] !=# '@') && l:mp[0][-1:] !=# '@'
      call add(l:refs, [l:mp[0], l:mp[1], l:mp[2]])
    endif
    let l:start = l:mp[2] + 2
  endwhile
endfunction
@

Navigation commands also need to know which chunk the cursor is
\emph{on}.  A definition claims its whole line---the natural target
of \enquote{go somewhere from here} is the header's chunk wherever
the cursor stands on that line.  Elsewhere the cursor must actually
be within a reference, brackets included.

<<[[s:chunk_at_cursor]]>>=
" The chunk definition or reference under the cursor, or ''.
function! s:chunk_at_cursor() abort
  let l:line = getline('.')
  let l:name = matchstr(l:line, s:DEF)
  if l:name !=# ''
    return l:name
  endif
  let l:cur = col('.') - 1
  for l:ref in s:refs_on_line(l:line)
    if l:cur >= l:ref[1] - 2 && l:cur < l:ref[2] + 2
      return l:ref[0]
    endif
  endfor
  return ''
endfunction
@

\subsubsection{The scan in Lua}
\label{sec:luascan}

The Neovim fast path is the plugin's sixth tangled file and its only
non--Vim-script one: Neovim adds every [[lua/]] directory on the
runtime path to Lua's module search, so the dispatcher reaches it as
[[require("noweb.scan")]].  The module returns the scan function
itself, and [[require]] caches the module, so the file is read once
per session.

The implementation mirrors \cref{sec:vimscan,sec:refsonline}
construct for construct---same state machine, same escape and
name-shape tests, same (line, column) output---and the test suite
compares the two implementations' output on the same buffer, so they
cannot drift apart silently.  Two dialect notes.  Lua patterns have
no alternation, so the skip test is a byte comparison ([[64]] is
[[@]]) plus a plain-text [[find]].  And Lua's lazy capture
[[(.-)]] plays the role of Vim's [[\{-}]]; the name-shape
conditions that Vim's pattern encodes as [[\%([^ ]\|...\)]] are
plain string tests here.

<<[[lua/noweb/scan.lua]]>>=
-- lua/noweb/scan.lua -- the occurrence scan, Neovim fast path.
--
-- Mirrors the Vim script scan in autoload/noweb.vim construct for
-- construct; the two must produce identical results (the test suite
-- compares them).  Returns {name = {defs = {{lnum, col}, ...},
--                                   uses = {{lnum, col}, ...}}}.

return function()
  local lines = vim.api.nvim_buf_get_lines(0, 0, -1, false)
  local occ = {}
  local function entry(name)
    local e = occ[name]
    if not e then
      e = { defs = {}, uses = {} }
      occ[name] = e
    end
    return e
  end
  local in_code = false
  for i, line in ipairs(lines) do
    -- the s:HOT skip test: only @-lines and bracket lines can matter
    if line:byte(1) == 64 or line:find('@<<', 1, true) then
      local name = line:match('^@<<(.-)@>>=%s*$')
      if name and name ~= '' then
        in_code = true
        table.insert(entry(name).defs, { i, 1 })
      elseif line == '@' or line:sub(1, 2) == '@ ' then
        in_code = false
      elseif in_code then
        <<collect the references on the line, in Lua>>
      end
    end
  end
  return occ
end
@

<<collect the references on the line, in Lua>>=
local init = 1
while true do
  local s, e, ref = line:find('@<<(.-)@>>', init)
  if not s then break end
  if ref ~= '' and ref:sub(1, 1) ~= ' ' and ref:sub(-1) ~= ' '
      and ref:sub(-1) ~= '@'
      and (s == 1 or line:sub(s - 1, s - 1) ~= '@') then
    table.insert(entry(ref).uses, { i, s })
  end
  init = e + 1
end
@

\subsection{Completing chunk names}
\label{sec:complete}

An omnifunc is called twice per completion
([[:h complete-functions]]): first with [[a:findstart]] set, to answer
\emph{where does the text being completed begin}, then with the text
accumulated so far as [[a:base]], to produce candidates.  Owning the
start column is what makes chunk names with blanks completable at
all---an identifier-based engine can never see past a space, which
is precisely why YCM's default identifier collector is no help for
chunk names and the omnifunc route is worth a section of its own.

Completion begins right after the last unescaped [[@<<]] before the
cursor.  If a [[>]] already intervenes, the reference is closed and
we are past it: returning $-3$ cancels quietly instead of beeping.

<<find the start of the chunk name being completed>>=
let l:before = strpart(getline('.'), 0, col('.') - 1)
let l:start = matchend(l:before, '.*@\@1<!@<<')
if l:start < 0 || stridx(l:before, '>', l:start) >= 0
  return -3
endif
return l:start
@

The candidates are every known chunk name---defined ones and
referenced-but-undefined ones alike.  Offering the undefined ones is
deliberate: writing the use before the definition is normal literate
style, and the [[menu]] column showing \enquote{undefined} against a
name doubles as typo detection.  Defined chunks show their append
count instead.

The order is context-dependent.  Completing at a \emph{definition}
position---the [[@<<]] opens the line---the user is about to define
or append, and the natural targets are exactly the names used
somewhere but not yet defined, so those come first; completing a use
mid-line, the defined names lead.  Both groups are always present,
alphabetical within.  Plain \texttt{CTRL-X CTRL-O} preserves this
order exactly; YCM re-ranks candidates with its own fuzzy matcher,
so there the ordering is best-effort and the [[menu]] tags carry the
signal instead.

Each candidate closes the reference for you: the inserted text is
[[name@>>]], unless the character after the cursor is already a
[[>]].  The popup stays readable through [[abbr]], which is what the
menu displays in place of the [[word]] actually inserted.

<<collect the matching chunk names>>=
let l:occ = s:chunk_occurrences()
let l:close = getline('.')[col('.') - 1] ==# '>' ? '' : '@>>'
" At a definition position (the @<< opens the line) the likely target
" is a name still lacking its definition; completing a use, defined
" names lead instead.
let l:defining = strpart(getline('.'), 0, col('.') - 1) =~# '^@<<[^>]*$'
let l:defined = []
let l:undefined = []
for l:name in sort(keys(l:occ))
  if stridx(l:name, a:base) != 0
    continue
  endif
  let l:n = len(l:occ[l:name].defs)
  call add(l:n == 0 ? l:undefined : l:defined, {
        \ 'word': l:name . l:close,
        \ 'abbr': l:name,
        \ 'menu': l:n == 0 ? 'undefined' : l:n == 1 ? 'def' : 'def+' . (l:n - 1),
        \ })
endfor
return l:defining ? l:undefined + l:defined : l:defined + l:undefined
@

<<[[noweb#complete]]>>=
" Omnifunc completing chunk names after @<< (:h complete-functions).
function! noweb#complete(findstart, base) abort
  if a:findstart
    <<find the start of the chunk name being completed>>
  endif
  <<collect the matching chunk names>>
endfunction
@

\subsection{Definitions are tags}
\label{sec:tagfunc}

Chunk definitions map onto Vim's tag model with almost suspicious
precision.  A chunk may be defined many times---each append is
another definition---and a tag may have many matches: so
\texttt{CTRL-]} on a reference jumps to the first definition,
[[:tnext]] steps through the appends, \texttt{CTRL-T} pops back up
the tag stack, and \texttt{g]} or [[:tselect]] lists all of them.  All of
that behaviour comes from the [['tagfunc']] option; none of it needs
a mapping.

Two details deserve their comment.  When the tagfunc is invoked by a
normal-mode command, [[a:flags]] contains [[c]] and the function may
consult the cursor---which we must, because the [[a:pattern]] Vim
passes is derived from the word under the cursor and chunk names may
contain blanks.  And each match locates its definition by \emph{line
number} rather than the conventional search pattern: every append of
a chunk shares the byte-identical header line, so a pattern could
only ever find the first one.

Returning [[v:null]] (rather than an empty list) when the cursor is
not on a chunk hands the lookup back to Vim's regular tags machinery,
so \texttt{CTRL-]} elsewhere in the buffer still searches any tags
file the user has.  An empty list would instead declare
\enquote{no such tag} ourselves---which is exactly right for a chunk
that is referenced but never defined.

<<[[noweb#tagfunc]]>>=
" Tagfunc resolving chunk names: every definition of the chunk is one
" tag match, in file order, so CTRL-] jumps to the first definition
" and :tnext steps through the appends.  Invoked by a normal-mode
" command ('c' in a:flags), the name comes from the cursor rather
" than a:pattern, which cannot hold the blanks chunk names may have.
function! noweb#tagfunc(pattern, flags, info) abort
  let l:name = stridx(a:flags, 'c') >= 0 ? s:chunk_at_cursor() : a:pattern
  let l:occ = s:chunk_occurrences()
  if l:name ==# ''
    return v:null
  endif
  if !has_key(l:occ, l:name)
    return stridx(a:flags, 'c') >= 0 ? [] : v:null
  endif
  let l:tags = []
  for l:def in l:occ[l:name].defs
    " The cmd is the definition's line number: appends share identical
    " header lines, so a search pattern could not tell them apart.
    call add(l:tags, {'name': l:name, 'filename': expand('%:p'),
          \ 'cmd': string(l:def[0]), 'kind': 'd'})
  endfor
  return l:tags
endfunction
@

\subsection{Stepping through uses and appends}
\label{sec:refs}

The motion jumps to the next or previous occurrence of the chunk
under the cursor---definition or use, the pattern matches both.
[[m']] first: the jump is a real jump, so \texttt{CTRL-O} and
[[``]] return to where it started.  The search wraps, making
\texttt{]c} from the last occurrence a quick way home to the first.

<<[[noweb#next_occurrence]]>>=
" The ]c / [c motion: the next (dir > 0) or previous occurrence --
" definition or use -- of the chunk under the cursor, wrapping.
function! noweb#next_occurrence(dir) abort
  let l:name = s:chunk_at_cursor()
  if l:name ==# ''
    call s:no_chunk()
    return
  endif
  normal! m'
  call search('@\@1<!@<<' . s:re_escape(l:name) . '@>>',
        \ a:dir > 0 ? 'w' : 'bw')
endfunction
@

[[:NowebRefs]] shows the whole picture at once: the location list
gets one entry per occurrence, definitions and uses in file order,
and the list window opens for browsing.  The location list rather
than the quickfix list because the survey is a per-window affair; a
second window surveying another chunk should not clobber it.

<<[[noweb#refs]]>>=
" Fill the location list with every definition and use of the chunk
" under the cursor, in file order, and open it.
function! noweb#refs() abort
  let l:name = s:chunk_at_cursor()
  if l:name ==# ''
    call s:no_chunk()
    return
  endif
  let l:occ = get(s:chunk_occurrences(), l:name, {'defs': [], 'uses': []})
  let l:places = sort(l:occ.defs + l:occ.uses,
        \ {a, b -> a[0] == b[0] ? a[1] - b[1] : a[0] - b[0]})
  let l:items = []
  for l:place in l:places
    call add(l:items, {'bufnr': bufnr('%'), 'lnum': l:place[0],
          \ 'col': l:place[1], 'text': getline(l:place[0])})
  endfor
  call setloclist(0, [], ' ', {'title': '@<<' . l:name . '@>>',
        \ 'items': l:items})
  lopen
endfunction

function! s:no_chunk() abort
  echohl WarningMsg
  echomsg 'noweb: no chunk name under the cursor'
  echohl None
endfunction
@

\section{Language intelligence in chunks}
\label{sec:shadow}

Completion and navigation (\cref{sec:navigation}) understand noweb's
\emph{structure}; this section is about understanding the
\emph{languages}.  A Python chunk should get Python diagnostics,
completion, hover documentation and goto-definition---the things a
language server provides---and the prose should get a full \TeX{}
toolchain.  The plugin cannot reimplement any of that, and following
the by now familiar principle, it does not try: it arranges for the
tools that already exist to see the code they expect.

The arrangement is an \emph{invisible tangled buffer} per root
chunk: a hidden buffer holding the real tangled program, kept in
sync with the noweb source, with a language server attached to it.
Every query---diagnostics, completion, a definition lookup---runs
against the shadow buffer, and every result is mapped back into the
[[.nw]] buffer.  Tangling for real, rather than extracting chunk
regions in place, is what makes the language server's view
\emph{honest}:
chunks appear in tangled order and at their spliced indentation, so
Python's indentation-sensitive parse and cross-chunk references both
work.  This is Neovim-only ([[vim.system]], [[vim.lsp]],
[[vim.diagnostic]]); on Vim the plugin simply keeps its
v1.6.1 behaviour.

The line mapping does not have to be invented: noweb's
\texttt{linemark} filter (from the author's noweb fork,
\texttt{contrib/dbosk}) exists precisely because [[notangle -L]]
cannot preserve indentation in languages like Python.  Run as
[[notangle -filter linemark]], it inserts a source-location comment
at the start of each code chunk and after each line that uses a
chunk---\texttt{\# 5 "foo.nw"}, with a configurable comment
leader---and tangles \emph{without} [[-L]], so the code itself is
untouched.  A marker announces the source line of the tangled line
that follows it; lines then advance in lockstep until the next
marker.  The markers stay in the shadow buffer (they are comments;
the language server reads past them) and \emph{are} the map: one
pass over the buffer recovers, for every tangled line, its source
line---unique, because every tangled line comes from exactly one
source line.  The forward direction is ambiguous when a chunk is
spliced more than once; the plugin then maps to the first splice.
Columns differ from the source only by the splice indentation, so
the column correction is the difference in leading whitespace.
(The companion \texttt{nolinemap} maps \emph{tool output} back the
same way; \cref{sec:nowebmake} uses it directly.)

\subsection{What the Lua side needs to know}

The shadow module needs two things the Vim script side already
computes: the chunk inventory (roots are chunks that are defined but
never used) and each chunk's language (\cref{sec:infer}).  Script's
locals stay local; two accessors export them.

<<[[noweb#occurrences]] and [[noweb#languages]] accessors>>=
" Accessors for the Lua shadow module (lua/noweb/shadow.lua): the
" occurrence inventory and the chunk-language map of this buffer.
function! noweb#occurrences() abort
  return s:chunk_occurrences()
endfunction

function! noweb#languages() abort
  return s:infer_languages()
endfunction
@

\subsection{The shadow module}
\label{sec:shadowmodule}

The module keeps, per noweb buffer, one shadow per typed root:
the hidden buffer, the two line maps, and the marker set.  Sync is
memoized on [[b:changedtick]] exactly like the occurrence scan.

<<[[lua/noweb/shadow.lua]]>>=
-- lua/noweb/shadow.lua -- invisible tangled buffers backing language
-- intelligence (diagnostics, completion, hover, goto) in code chunks.
--
-- One shadow per typed root chunk: a hidden buffer holding
--   notangle -t8 -filter "linemark -c <leader>" -R<root>
-- of the live .nw buffer.  The linemark markers stay in the buffer
-- and provide the line maps; language servers attach to the shadow
-- and results map back into the .nw buffer.

local M = {}

-- state[nwbuf] = { tick = changedtick, roots = { [root] = shadow } }
-- shadow = { buf, root, lang, cfg, ns,
--            src = { tangled line -> source line },
--            fwd = { source line -> first tangled line },
--            marker = { tangled line -> true } }
local state = {}

-- NB: the skeleton splices the diagnostics chunk before the buffer
-- chunk, although the document presents them the other way around:
-- the autocmd closure in ensure_shadow captures forward_diagnostics
-- as an upvalue, which requires the local to exist first.

<<shadow: language configuration>>

<<shadow: discover the typed roots>>

<<shadow: tangle one root with markers>>

<<shadow: parse the markers into line maps>>

<<shadow: forward diagnostics into the noweb buffer>>

<<shadow: the hidden buffer and its language server>>

<<shadow: sync>>

<<shadow: locate a source position in a shadow>>

<<shadow: setup>>

return M
@

Languages are described by a comment leader (for linemark), a
filetype (set on the shadow buffer: it names the language to the LSP
client and lets that language's own plugins---lean.nvim, for
one---attach their machinery), and optionally a language-server
command.  The keys are Vim syntax names, the same names the language
inference produces.  [[g:noweb_languages]] extends or overrides the
defaults.

<<shadow: language configuration>>=
local defaults = {
  python = { leader = '#', filetype = 'python',
             lsp = { 'jedi-language-server' } },
  lean   = { leader = '--', filetype = 'lean' },
  lua    = { leader = '--', filetype = 'lua' },
}

local function language_config()
  return vim.tbl_deep_extend('force', defaults, vim.g.noweb_languages or {})
end
@

A root is a chunk that is defined but never used; it gets a shadow
when its inferred language has a configuration.  Both facts come
from the Vim script side through the accessors, so there is exactly
one inventory and one inference.

<<shadow: discover the typed roots>>=
local function typed_roots(nwbuf)
  local occ, langs
  vim.api.nvim_buf_call(nwbuf, function()
    occ = vim.fn['noweb#occurrences']()
    langs = vim.fn['noweb#languages']()
  end)
  local cfg = language_config()
  local roots = {}
  for name, entry in pairs(occ) do
    if #entry.defs > 0 and #entry.uses == 0 then
      local lang = langs[name]
      if lang and cfg[lang] then
        roots[name] = { lang = lang, cfg = cfg[lang] }
      end
    end
  end
  return roots
end
@

Tangling feeds the \emph{live buffer} to notangle on stdin, so
unsaved chunks are part of the shadow the moment they are typed.
The leader is single-quoted inside the filter string because
notangle hands the filter to a shell (an unquoted [[#]] would start
a comment there).  The call is synchronous; notangle on a large
source is a few milliseconds, and sync is memoized and debounced.

<<shadow: tangle one root with markers>>=
local function tangle(nwbuf, root, leader)
  local lines = vim.api.nvim_buf_get_lines(nwbuf, 0, -1, false)
  local res = vim.system({
    'notangle', '-t8',
    '-filter', "linemark -c '" .. leader .. "'",
    '-R' .. root,
  }, { stdin = table.concat(lines, '\n') .. '\n', text = true }):wait(5000)
  if not res or res.code ~= 0 then
    return nil
  end
  local out = vim.split(res.stdout or '', '\n')
  if out[#out] == '' then
    table.remove(out)
  end
  return out
end
@

A marker line announces the source line of the tangled line that
follows; both the marker and that line map to it, and subsequent
lines count up until the next marker.  Markers are indented with
their splice, hence the leading-whitespace allowance.

<<shadow: parse the markers into line maps>>=
local function parse_maps(lines, leader)
  local pat = '^%s*' .. vim.pesc(leader) .. ' (%d+) "'
  local src, fwd, marker = {}, {}, {}
  local cur = nil
  for t, line in ipairs(lines) do
    local announced = line:match(pat)
    if announced then
      cur = tonumber(announced)
      marker[t] = true
      src[t] = cur
    elseif cur then
      src[t] = cur
      if not fwd[cur] then
        fwd[cur] = t
      end
      cur = cur + 1
    end
  end
  return src, fwd, marker
end
@

The shadow buffer is an unlisted scratch buffer named after the
source and the root, so buffer lists and language servers can tell
shadows apart.  Setting its filetype is deliberately more than
cosmetic: the LSP client derives the document's language from it,
and the language's own ftplugins run on the hidden buffer.  The
server, when configured, is started with the noweb file's directory
as root; [[vim.lsp.start]] reuses a running client for the same
name and root.

<<shadow: the hidden buffer and its language server>>=
local function shadow_name(nwbuf, root)
  local nwname = vim.api.nvim_buf_get_name(nwbuf)
  return nwname .. '.shadow/' .. root:gsub('[%[%]]', '')
end

local function ensure_shadow(nwbuf, root, lang, cfg)
  local st = state[nwbuf]
  local sh = st.roots[root]
  if not (sh and vim.api.nvim_buf_is_valid(sh.buf)) then
    local buf = vim.api.nvim_create_buf(false, true)
    vim.api.nvim_buf_set_name(buf, shadow_name(nwbuf, root))
    sh = { buf = buf, root = root, lang = lang, cfg = cfg,
           ns = vim.api.nvim_create_namespace('noweb-shadow:' .. root) }
    st.roots[root] = sh
    vim.bo[buf].filetype = cfg.filetype
    if cfg.lsp then
      vim.lsp.start({
        name = 'noweb-' .. lang,
        cmd = cfg.lsp,
        root_dir = vim.fs.dirname(vim.api.nvim_buf_get_name(nwbuf)),
      }, { bufnr = buf })
    end
    <<shadow: forward this shadow's diagnostics>>
  end
  return sh
end
@

Diagnostics translate line by line: the diagnostic's tangled line
looks up its source line, the column moves by the difference in
leading whitespace (the splice indent), and diagnostics on marker
lines---lines the author never wrote---are dropped.  The translated
set is published on the noweb buffer in the shadow's own namespace,
so several roots coexist.

<<shadow: forward diagnostics into the noweb buffer>>=
local function indent_width(line)
  return #(line:match('^%s*') or '')
end

local function col_delta(sh, t, nwbuf)
  local tline = (vim.api.nvim_buf_get_lines(sh.buf, t - 1, t, false)[1]) or ''
  local nline = (vim.api.nvim_buf_get_lines(
    nwbuf, sh.src[t] - 1, sh.src[t], false)[1]) or ''
  return indent_width(tline) - indent_width(nline)
end

local function forward_diagnostics(nwbuf, sh)
  local out = {}
  for _, d in ipairs(vim.diagnostic.get(sh.buf)) do
    local t = d.lnum + 1
    if sh.src[t] and not sh.marker[t] then
      local delta = col_delta(sh, t, nwbuf)
      local nd = vim.tbl_extend('force', {}, d)
      nd.bufnr = nwbuf
      nd.lnum = sh.src[t] - 1
      nd.col = math.max(0, d.col - delta)
      if nd.end_lnum and sh.src[nd.end_lnum + 1] then
        nd.end_lnum = sh.src[nd.end_lnum + 1] - 1
        nd.end_col = math.max(0, (nd.end_col or 0) - delta)
      else
        nd.end_lnum = nd.lnum
        nd.end_col = nd.col
      end
      table.insert(out, nd)
    end
  end
  vim.diagnostic.set(sh.ns, nwbuf, out)
end
@

<<shadow: forward this shadow's diagnostics>>=
vim.api.nvim_create_autocmd('DiagnosticChanged', {
  buffer = sh.buf,
  callback = function()
    if vim.api.nvim_buf_is_valid(nwbuf) then
      forward_diagnostics(nwbuf, sh)
    end
  end,
})
@

Sync re-tangles every typed root whose buffer changed since the last
sync, updates the shadow buffers (which sends the language servers
their [[didChange]]), rebuilds the maps, and drops shadows whose
root disappeared.

<<shadow: sync>>=
function M.sync(nwbuf)
  nwbuf = (nwbuf and nwbuf ~= 0) and nwbuf or vim.api.nvim_get_current_buf()
  local st = state[nwbuf]
  if not st then
    return nil
  end
  local tick = vim.api.nvim_buf_get_changedtick(nwbuf)
  if st.tick == tick then
    return st
  end
  st.tick = tick
  local roots = typed_roots(nwbuf)
  for root, sh in pairs(st.roots) do
    if not roots[root] then
      vim.diagnostic.reset(sh.ns, nwbuf)
      pcall(vim.api.nvim_buf_delete, sh.buf, { force = true })
      st.roots[root] = nil
    end
  end
  for root, info in pairs(roots) do
    local lines = tangle(nwbuf, root, info.cfg.leader)
    if lines then
      local sh = ensure_shadow(nwbuf, root, info.lang, info.cfg)
      sh.src, sh.fwd, sh.marker = parse_maps(lines, info.cfg.leader)
      local old = vim.api.nvim_buf_get_lines(sh.buf, 0, -1, false)
      if not vim.deep_equal(old, lines) then
        vim.api.nvim_buf_set_lines(sh.buf, 0, -1, false, lines)
      end
      forward_diagnostics(nwbuf, sh)
    end
  end
  return st
end
@

Queries need to know which shadow, if any, holds a given source
line, and where.  Prose lines are in no shadow; a line of a chunk
spliced several times maps to its first splice.

<<shadow: locate a source position in a shadow>>=
-- Locate source line lnum: returns shadow, tangled line, column delta.
function M.locate(nwbuf, lnum)
  nwbuf = (nwbuf and nwbuf ~= 0) and nwbuf or vim.api.nvim_get_current_buf()
  local st = M.sync(nwbuf)
  if not st then
    return nil
  end
  for _, sh in pairs(st.roots) do
    local t = sh.fwd[lnum]
    if t then
      return sh, t, col_delta(sh, t, nwbuf)
    end
  end
  return nil
end
@

Setup runs once per noweb buffer, from the ftplugin: an initial
sync, a debounced re-sync on edits (leaving insert mode or any
change in normal mode), and cleanup when the buffer goes away.  The
debounce keeps diagnostics live without tangling on every
keystroke; queries call [[M.sync]] themselves, so they never see a
stale shadow regardless of the timer.

<<shadow: setup>>=
function M.setup(nwbuf)
  nwbuf = (nwbuf and nwbuf ~= 0) and nwbuf or vim.api.nvim_get_current_buf()
  if state[nwbuf] then
    return
  end
  state[nwbuf] = { tick = -1, roots = {} }
  local group = vim.api.nvim_create_augroup('noweb-shadow:' .. nwbuf, {})
  local pending = false
  vim.api.nvim_create_autocmd({ 'TextChanged', 'InsertLeave' }, {
    group = group,
    buffer = nwbuf,
    callback = function()
      if not pending then
        pending = true
        vim.defer_fn(function()
          pending = false
          if vim.api.nvim_buf_is_valid(nwbuf) then
            M.sync(nwbuf)
          end
        end, 300)
      end
    end,
  })
  vim.api.nvim_create_autocmd('BufWipeout', {
    group = group,
    buffer = nwbuf,
    callback = function()
      for _, sh in pairs(state[nwbuf].roots) do
        pcall(vim.api.nvim_buf_delete, sh.buf, { force = true })
      end
      state[nwbuf] = nil
      vim.api.nvim_del_augroup_by_id(group)
    end,
  })
  M.sync(nwbuf)
end
@

\subsection{Hooking the shadows into the buffer}

The ftplugin starts the machinery on Neovim unless the user opts
out; everything degrades gracefully when notangle (or linemark) is
missing---the module simply produces no shadows, and the plugin is
back to its v1.6.1 feature set.

<<start the shadow machinery>>=
if has('nvim') && get(g:, 'noweb_shadow', 1) && executable('notangle')
  lua require('noweb.shadow').setup(0)
endif
@

\section{Building and maintaining}
\label{sec:building}

Editing this file follows the usual literate loop: edit
[[vim-noweb.nw]], run [[make]], and commit the [[.nw]] together with
the re-tangled files.  [[make check]] enforces the two things worth
enforcing: [[noroots]] must report exactly the intended root chunks
(an extra root is a typo'd chunk reference), and the tangled files
must match the working tree.

